{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/196140-fetch-and-un-natural-death","id":196140,"title":"Fetch and (un)natural death","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-13T01:52:43-07:00","id":196140,"title":"Fetch and (un)natural death","content":"So, a fetch dies its own death. It has a heart attack, or dies in a car crash, or slips on a patch of black ice and cracks its head open on a curb... And then falls to pieces in a few minutes/hours/days.<br /> <br /> What that looks like to me, is that essentially fetch deaths are either misssing persons (people just dying and disappearing into bits and pieces without other people seeing them) or cases of body theft (a fetch dies, is declared dead and then crumbles to composite parts in the morgue/mortuary/etc). On the upper side of 'a few days' a fetch might have enough time to get buried, but I don't imagine it happening too often.<br /> <br /> Given that the stated purpose of fetches is to make sure people don't mysteriously disappear and attract attention, how does that work?<br /> <br /> The only thing I can think of is that the Wyrd somehow works in a way to make fetches die in a way that doesn't demand a body. Auntie Ally's fetch died in a plane crash, that might work. Drowning too. But other than that I can't think of a way a person might die that will conceal their fetch nature. Not to mention &quot;the Wyrd does it&quot; doesn't sound too convincing to me.<br /> <br /> Has anyone else thought about it? How do you deal with fetches dying without the changeling's involvement?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3316,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3316-cantshutup","user":"cantshutup","date":". 07-13-2014, 04:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3316-cantshutup","name":"cantshutup","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T06:55:15-07:00","id":197881,"title":"","content":"Fetch reversion is explicitly timed to plot convenience. No small number of placeholders will only revert to the materials they're made from after they're actually buried.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T18:44:28-07:00","id":198445,"title":"","content":"Although, it should be said that some Fetches do naturally die in a manner that their bodies aren't recovered. This does essentially leave missing people reports, which can be either taken as the Others not giving two cents about what happens to the Fetch so long as it doesn't lead back to them or the fact that the Gentry are just as fallible as any other creature in the World of Darkness. Although I still agree with <b>Satchel</b> that most things related to Arcadia primarily work under the laws of plot convenience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}